# Barn owl

The barn owl is a pale, long-winged owl found on every continent except
Antarctica. It is abroad between dusk and dawn, quartering rough pasture at
walking pace a few metres up and dropping onto voles and mice located by ear
alone. The facial disc funnels sound toward asymmetric ear openings, and the
fringed leading edge of the wing breaks up the airflow so that the approach is
inaudible to prey and to a listener alike. By day it roosts in a hollow tree, a
ruin or a church tower, which is where the English name comes from. See
[[tytonidae]] and [[strigiformes]].
